Subregion plan: This region is the timber residence itself, a single-story post-and-beam structure raised roughly 0.6 m off the ground on a stone-and-timber foundation, capped by a steep dark tiled hip-and-gable roof with deep overhanging eaves that account for the upper portion of the bounding box. The exterior wall plane is composed of exposed natural wood posts and beams infilled with white plaster panels and sliding paper shoji screens, and a narrow wooden engawa veranda runs along the garden-facing sides (those facing the refined planting toward the south and west of the grounds), sitting at the raised floor level and reached by a step down to the garden. The footprint is organized as a sequence of tatami-matted interior rooms separated by sliding partitions and laid out along the long axis: a service and entry area with a lowered genkan floor where footwear is removed, positioned on the side nearest the approaching gate and entry path; one or more formal reception rooms with a recessed tokonoma alcove placed so they open through shoji onto the engawa and the most composed part of the garden; and more private living and sleeping rooms set deeper and more enclosed. One modest wing reads as slightly raised relative to the rest. Interior circulation flows linearly from the entry through the room sequence, with the engawa providing an outer circulation edge between polished interior and garden. Downstream steps should subdivide the interior into these distinct rooms and resolve the structural shell, roof, foundation, and veranda; the overall character is restrained and ordered, with proportion and visible joinery rather than applied ornament. The major distinct areas are the entry/service zone, the formal reception zone, and the private living/sleeping zone, each warranting its own planning, alongside the enveloping structure and engawa.


