Subregion plan: This region is the walled grounds that surround the raised timber house, occupying the full 32 m by 32 m plot at ground level (grade 0, with the house sitting roughly 0.6 m above on its foundation). The house is set off-center toward the north and slightly east, so the open garden is largest on the south side (the area beyond the front engawa and shoji of the formal reception room, Z increasing away from the house), substantial on the west side (beyond the western engawa and the private day room shoji), narrow along the rear north edge, and present as an approach strip on the east entry side. The entire plot is bounded by a low plastered perimeter wall with tiled coping running along all four edges, broken by a tiled wooden gate on the east side roughly aligned with the genkan entrance. The garden divides into a few clearly distinct functional areas, each warranting its own treatment. First and most important is the refined composed landscape on the south and southwest, directly facing the formal reception room and the engawa it opens onto: this is where the design concentrates a small koi pond crossed by an arched element and fed by a trickling water feature, raked gravel and moss ground surfaces, stepping-stone paths, clipped low shrubs, stone lanterns, and a few mature trees positioned to frame the views from the veranda. This is the showpiece of the grounds and reads as the most deliberately arranged and immaculate. Second is the entry approach on the east side, running from the gate inward to the genkan entrance: a path of stepping stones or fitted paving across plainer ground, with restrained planting flanking the arrival route, functioning as the practical arrival sequence rather than a contemplative composition. Third is the surrounding outer grounds along the west and rear north edges and the perimeter itself: simpler, more naturalistic ground cover of moss and gravel, additional mature framing trees, and the enclosing wall, quieter and less manicured than the south garden, screening the property and giving the inner compositions their backdrop. The ground transitions between the raised house floor and the garden grade happen at the engawa-facing edges via stepping stones down. Downstream steps should establish these distinct areas as subzones, keeping the most refined water-and-stone composition on the south/southwest facing the formal rooms, the arrival path and gate on the east, and the plainer planted grounds and perimeter wall wrapping the remainder.

