Subregion plan: This region is the western planted grounds of the estate, a roughly 11 m (east-west) by 16 m (north-south) ground-level area at grade 0, lying west of the raised timber house whose floor sits about 0.6 m above this grade. Its eastern edge runs against the west engawa veranda and the garden-facing shoji of the private day/sitting room, which form this zone's single controlled outlook; its western edge is the low plastered perimeter wall enclosing the property, and it extends the full depth from the rear north corner toward the south garden boundary. The ground is worked as a quieter, more naturalistic landscape than the refined south showpiece garden: broad continuous cover of raked fine gravel and patches and low mounds of moss over packed earth, kept simpler and less densely composed, without a water feature or dense ornamental arrangement. A few mature trees stand toward the western perimeter wall, their canopies positioned to screen the boundary and to frame the limited view seen from the day room shoji and engawa, so the trees concentrate along the outer edge while the ground nearer the house stays more open. Use is contemplative and passive rather than circulated: this is the garden the private rooms look out onto rather than a strolled showpiece, so the surface stays mostly open swept gravel and moss with restrained low planting, and any minimal stone accents or a short run of stepping stones belong near the engawa edge where the floor steps down to grade. The composition is asymmetric and finely maintained but deliberately plainer toward the far corners. Downstream object placement has latitude over the exact number and placement of trees, the distribution of moss and gravel areas, and any low stone accents, keeping the framing trees toward the western wall and the ground near the engawa more open as the outlook from the private rooms.

