                Plan for this region: This region is the principal composed garden of the estate, a roughly 32 m (east-west) by 16 m (north-south) ground-level landscape lying directly south of the house front, beginning at the raised front engawa and the broad garden stepping stone (along its north edge, where the house floor sits about 0.6 m above grade) and extending south to the low plastered perimeter wall. It is the most deliberately arranged and immaculate ground of the property, intended to be read as a single composed scene viewed from the formal reception room and the engawa it opens onto, which face it across the room's centered span (roughly the central portion of this region's width). Ground level is grade 0 throughout, with the surface worked into a designed landscape rather than flat lawn: broad areas of raked fine gravel forming the open swept ground, patches and low mounds of moss, and groupings of clipped low shrubs, arranged asymmetrically rather than symmetrically. The compositional centerpiece is a small irregular koi pond set into the ground somewhat off-center and placed so it sits within the view from the reception engawa; it is crossed by a low arched element spanning between its banks, and fed at one end by a trickling water feature descending over stone from slightly higher worked ground, so water enters at the upstream side and the pond holds still water below. A path of stepping stones leads outward from the broad garden step at the engawa into the garden, threading past the pond and the planted areas and connecting the viewing edge to the deeper parts of the composition. Stone lanterns are positioned as low vertical accents at a few considered points, such as near a pond bank, beside the path, or at a turn, rather than lined up regularly. A few mature framing trees stand toward the southern and outer edges near the perimeter wall, their canopies positioned to frame and enclose the views from the veranda and to screen the boundary, with 